From 0324d66f26e5922ae7ffb5a2dca37f1435178433 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 17:43:29 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] inline-3d(#117): never weave a region the page still owns,
 and say WHY a tile did not join
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Replaces the first 0081, which FAILED on hardware (composition sample, box run
32533836796): "a heavy blurred veil on ALL the page, 3d assets either black or
SBS underneath". This one is the small, provably-inert half of it plus the
instrumentation that would have prevented the mistake.

WHAT THE FAILING SITTING PROVED.

The pre-declared falsification fired: `CHILD-PASS join` count 0 across the
whole session, `tiles_in_child_pass=0` on every plane-split line, while
`tiles_without_k=1` persisted. So the cross-pass join never engaged, and the
entire draw-time half of that patch was INERT on the page that failed. It was
a fix for a condition that page does not have.

The new fallback counters say what the condition actually is. Steady state:

  ID-MATCH FALLBACK: … target=878,698 796x796 among 41 candidate quads
    (7 in child passes, 1 weavable passes, 0 inadmissible id hits)

"1 weavable passes" is the root pass alone — every child pass touching a tile
on that page is a backdrop-FILTER pass (the sample's .frostfull / .frosthalf /
frosted header / .frostbadge), which the weavability gate refuses correctly
because a root-space blit cannot reproduce a blur. And "0 inadmissible id
hits" is the decisive number: NO quad anywhere in the frame — root pass, child
pass, admissible or not — carries that element's layer id. The tile's layer
emitted no SharedQuadState at all. No join, cross-pass or otherwise, could
ever have matched it.

So #117's third mechanism, as diagnosed, is not what that page suffers from,
and the cross-pass join has zero hardware evidence behind it. It is withdrawn
rather than shipped on a hypothesis. It is preserved verbatim in this repo's
history (cr151 commit 17a2d4f, patch file at browser-wt-117 commit d30ec89)
and can return the moment a log line justifies it.

THE VEIL, CONCRETELY — AND IT IS NOT NEW.

The mechanism is D-prime (0068) meeting a page built to stress it, and the
sample states the contract it expects in its own markup (case 02: "the frost
itself is crisp, and the region behind it reads FLAT"). What it gets is not
flat, it is empty:

  * Display::DrawAndSwap suppresses each joined tile's canvas resource from the
    page raster — that is what lets the weave own those pixels;
  * ComputeInline3dPlaneSplit records, for every backdrop-filter RPDQ over a
    tile, `own_rect ∩ tile_rect` into inline3d_frosted_rects_;
  * phase (B) clips the woven draw-back out of each of those rects
    (SkClipOp::kDifference).

Inside a frost rect the frame therefore has NEITHER the canvas (suppressed)
NOR the weave (clipped). It has whatever was behind the canvas — and the
sample's stages are deliberately bare ("A stage is VISUALLY BARE: no
background, border, radius or overflow clip"), so what the blur samples is the
flat page background. Dark. That is the reported "3d assets black", and the
un-joined tile beside it is the reported "SBS".

At the density this was designed against — the gallery's single frosted header
band, `frosted=1` — the withheld strip was thin and sat over page chrome, so
nobody saw it. This page runs `frosted=8` with `.frostfull{inset:-10px}`
covering three entire tiles at once, so every covered asset goes dark and the
page reads as veiled. The first 0081's own commit message asserted the
opposite ("it is not a hole: suppression exposes the page background still
drawn beneath it") — true, and irrelevant: when the frost covers the WHOLE
tile, the page background IS the whole tile.

That defect predates both 0080 and 0081 and is not addressed here. Fixing it
means giving the frost something real to blur — a mono draw where the weave is
withheld — or not withholding the weave when the frost covers most of a tile.
Both are D-prime design changes and neither belongs in a #117 patch.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES.

(1) WEAVE SUBMISSION MOVES IN LOCKSTEP WITH PAGE PREPARATION (display.cc).

Display::DrawAndSwap submitted a weave rect for EVERY tracked rect,
unconditionally — including tiles it had just failed to resolve and had
therefore NOT suppressed from the page raster. Those rects still hold ordinary
page pixels; for a tile straddling the top of the viewport, clipped to a 198px
strip, those pixels are the site's sticky header. The runtime was told to
weave it, and did: the gallery's title came back interlaced. That is content
with nothing to do with inline-3D being corrupted by it.

It is the exact inverse of web#12's "never weave the hole" law, and it slips
past that law's own guard: the GPU-side check refuses to SNAPSHOT such a tile,
but 0074's keep-previous rescue then re-submits the rect from inside that very
branch, so it reached the runtime's batch anyway.

A rect is now submitted only if this frame prepared it: the tile has a
resolved canvas layer, OR nothing resolved at all this frame (in which case no
canvas quad was suppressed and snapshotting the page is the legitimate
pre-browser#18 weave source). That is precisely the predicate the GPU stage
already applies one stage later; moving it EARLIER is what stops the rect
reaching the batch at all.

KNOWN TRADE, stated rather than discovered later: a tile that loses its
resource can no longer be rescued by 0074's keep-previous, because its rect is
not in the batch to be kept. It shows the page's own drawing instead of a
stale stereo frame. That is the same "strictly better failure" web#12 chose —
the page draws the tile exactly as a page with no inline-3D would — and it is
the only direction that guarantees the runtime never weaves page chrome.
Withholding is counted at LOG(ERROR) on 0073's discipline, because it is also
the moment a tile stops weaving and must never be silent. On the composition
sample it fires for 1 of 5 tiles in steady state and 3 of 4 at some scroll
positions, which is a real amount of lost weaving and exactly why it is loud.

(2) THE FALLBACK MARKER STOPS GUESSING (surface_aggregator.{h,cc}).

"layer_id stamped but no root quad carries it (canvas quad occluded, in a
child pass, or resource not ready)" bundled three situations needing opposite
follow-ups behind one string, and the withdrawn patch picked the wrong one.
The marker now splits them on evidence:

  * elsewhere > 0 — the element IS drawn, by a quad in a pass this join does
    not search. A join problem; the pass id names the isolation.
  * elsewhere = 0 — NOTHING in the frame carries this layer id. The layer
    emitted no SharedQuadState, so no join could match it: not in the drawn
    layer list, or the resource is not ready. A cc/producer problem.

To answer that question the aggregator has to have LOOKED outside the root
resolved pass, so it now records those quads too — and |from_root_resolved_pass|
is what keeps this strictly an observability change. Both joins skip any
candidate without that flag, so the admitted pool is the EXACT pool the old
recording condition produced and the matched set is bit-identical to 0080.
Verified by inspection of the diff: the only non-comment changes in the joins
are the two `if (!cand.from_root_resolved_pass) continue;` guards. The
diagnostic entries are capped at 512.

Files:
  components/viz/service/display/display.cc
  components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.{h,cc}

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 components/viz/service/display/display.cc     | 57 +++++++++++++
 .../viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc | 81 +++++++++++++++++--
 .../viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.h  | 15 ++++
 3 files changed, 147 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/components/viz/service/display/display.cc b/components/viz/service/display/display.cc
index d652a9f..f31982f 100644
--- a/components/viz/service/display/display.cc
+++ b/components/viz/service/display/display.cc
@@ -1055,11 +1055,68 @@ bool Display::DrawAndSwap(const DrawAndSwapParams& params) {
     if (skia_output_surface_) {
       // Phase-1 identity: the GPU stage keys its per-target weave state by the
       // element token, so carry {rect, id} rather than bare rects.
+      //
+      // (browser#117) NEVER WEAVE A REGION YOU DID NOT PREPARE. This list used
+      // to be every tracked rect, unconditionally. That is the inverse of
+      // web#12's "never weave the hole" law and it is just as damaging: a tile
+      // whose canvas quad could not be resolved this frame is NOT suppressed
+      // from the page raster, so its rect still holds ordinary page pixels —
+      // and on a tile straddling the top of the viewport those pixels are the
+      // site's own sticky header. Submitting the rect anyway told the runtime
+      // to weave the header: the title came back interlaced (2D chrome pushed
+      // through the lenticular lattice) while the unsuppressed remainder of the
+      // tile below showed its raw side-by-side buffer. Doubly wrong, and the
+      // half that corrupts UNRELATED content is the worse half.
+      //
+      // So weave submission and page preparation move in LOCKSTEP: a rect is
+      // submitted only if this frame prepared it. "Prepared" is exactly the
+      // predicate the GPU stage already applies one stage later
+      // (skia_output_surface_impl_on_gpu.cc, "NEVER WEAVE THE HOLE") — the tile
+      // has a resolved canvas layer, OR nothing at all resolved this frame, in
+      // which case no canvas quad was suppressed and snapshotting the page is
+      // the legitimate pre-browser#18 weave source. Moving the same test
+      // EARLIER is what stops the rect reaching the runtime's batch at all,
+      // which the GPU-side skip could not do on its own: 0074's keep-previous
+      // rescue re-submits the rect from that very branch.
+      //
+      // The tile loses nothing it had. It is not suppressed, so the page draws
+      // it exactly as a page with no inline-3D would — the same "strictly
+      // better failure" web#12 chose, now applied to the submission too.
       std::vector<SkiaOutputSurface::Inline3dRect> weave_rects;
       weave_rects.reserve(frame.inline_3d_rects.size());
+      int unprepared = 0;
       for (const auto& r : frame.inline_3d_rects) {
+        bool prepared = resolved_canvas.empty();
+        for (const auto& layer : resolved_canvas) {
+          // Identity where there is one; the legacy zero-id channel has only
+          // geometry, and Display's own tile join above uses the same pair of
+          // tests in the same order.
+          if (!r.id.is_zero() ? layer.id == r.id : layer.rect == r.rect) {
+            prepared = true;
+            break;
+          }
+        }
+        if (!prepared) {
+          ++unprepared;
+          continue;
+        }
         weave_rects.push_back({r.rect, r.id});
       }
+      if (unprepared > 0) {
+        // LOG(ERROR) on 0073's discipline (first 3, then 1-in-300): withholding
+        // a rect is the correct outcome but it is also the moment the tile
+        // stops weaving, so it must never be silent.
+        static int s_unprepared = 0;
+        const int n = s_unprepared++;
+        if (n < 3 || (n % 300) == 0) {
+          LOG(ERROR) << "[DisplayXR] inline-3D withheld " << unprepared
+                     << " of " << frame.inline_3d_rects.size()
+                     << " weave rects: no canvas resource resolved, so the page"
+                        " still owns those pixels and the weave must not touch"
+                        " them (withheld="
+                     << (n + 1) << ")";
+        }
+      }
       skia_output_surface_->SetInline3DRects(std::move(weave_rects),
                                              std::move(resolved_canvas));
     }
diff --git a/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc b/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc
index 432ac77..7f67d81 100644
--- a/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc
+++ b/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.cc
@@ -1491,8 +1491,28 @@ void SurfaceAggregator::CopyQuadsToPass(
         // emits such a quad whose SharedImage is that layer's isolated content.
         // At Aggregate() end the ones matching an inline-3D canvas rect are the
         // isolated canvas resources the weave stage feeds as weave INPUT.
-        if (resolved_pass.is_root() &&
-            quad_data.remapped_resource_id != kInvalidResourceId) {
+        //
+        // browser#117: also record quads from NON-root resolved passes, purely
+        // so the fallback marker below can tell "no quad anywhere carries this
+        // element's layer id" apart from "a quad does carry it, but in a pass
+        // this join does not search". Those two need opposite follow-ups and
+        // were indistinguishable, which is what made the first attempt at this
+        // issue chase the wrong one.
+        //
+        // DIAGNOSTIC ONLY, and the |from_root_resolved_pass| flag is what keeps
+        // it so: every join below admits candidates on that flag, i.e. on the
+        // EXACT predicate this condition used before, so the matched set is
+        // bit-identical to the 0080 build. Nothing here changes a pixel — it
+        // only makes an existing ambiguity observable. Making the join actually
+        // follow the identity across passes is a separate, unproven change and
+        // is deliberately NOT in this patch.
+        const bool from_root_resolved_pass = resolved_pass.is_root();
+        if (quad_data.remapped_resource_id != kInvalidResourceId &&
+            (from_root_resolved_pass ||
+             cross_pass_diag_quads_ < kMaxCrossPassDiagQuads)) {
+          if (!from_root_resolved_pass) {
+            ++cross_pass_diag_quads_;
+          }
           // Map quad->rect (quad content space) to ROOT space: first the quad's
           // SharedQuadState quad_to_target_transform (→ dest pass target), then
           // the dest pass's transform_to_root_target. Without the SQS transform
@@ -1533,7 +1553,8 @@ void SurfaceAggregator::CopyQuadsToPass(
                                                      quad->visible_rect),
                uv, dest_sqs ? dest_sqs->layer_id : 0u,
                dest_sqs ? dest_sqs->layer_namespace_id
-                        : std::pair<uint32_t, uint32_t>()});
+                        : std::pair<uint32_t, uint32_t>(),
+               dest_pass->id, from_root_resolved_pass});
         }
       }
     }
@@ -2213,6 +2234,7 @@ AggregatedFrame SurfaceAggregator::Aggregate(
   tracked_element_rects_ = {};
   inline_3d_rects_ = {};
   root_resource_quads_ = {};
+  cross_pass_diag_quads_ = 0;
 
   ResolvedFrameData* resolved_frame = GetResolvedFrame(surface_id);
 
@@ -2487,6 +2509,12 @@ AggregatedFrame SurfaceAggregator::Aggregate(
     // when exactly one candidate qualifies — the slow-scroll/steady case.
     int64_t best_score = -1;
     for (const RootResourceQuad& cand : quads) {
+      // browser#117: candidates recorded for DIAGNOSIS ONLY are not join
+      // candidates. This restores the exact pool the recording condition used
+      // to produce, so widening that condition cannot move a single match.
+      if (!cand.from_root_resolved_pass) {
+        continue;
+      }
       const gfx::Rect& qr = cand.rect;
       if (qr.IsEmpty()) {
         continue;
@@ -2535,6 +2563,10 @@ AggregatedFrame SurfaceAggregator::Aggregate(
     }
     std::optional<RootResourceQuad> found;
     for (const RootResourceQuad& cand : quads) {
+      // browser#117: diagnostic-only entries never win a join — see best_match.
+      if (!cand.from_root_resolved_pass) {
+        continue;
+      }
       if (cand.sqs_layer_id == target.sqs_layer_id &&
           cand.layer_namespace_id == target.layer_namespace_id) {
         // LAST match wins, matching best_match's tie policy: one layer can
@@ -2668,19 +2700,56 @@ AggregatedFrame SurfaceAggregator::Aggregate(
       // different follow-ups, and telling them apart from a log is the whole
       // value of the marker. Throttled: a persistent fallback would otherwise
       // spam at frame rate.
+      //
+      // browser#117: "no root quad carries it" used to bundle three very
+      // different situations behind one string — occluded, in a child pass, or
+      // resource not ready — and the first attempt at this issue picked the
+      // wrong one and shipped a fix for a condition the failing page did not
+      // have. Split it on evidence instead. |elsewhere| is the number of quads
+      // that DO carry this element's (namespace, layer id) but were recorded
+      // only for diagnosis, i.e. live in a pass this join does not search.
+      //
+      //   elsewhere > 0 → the element IS being drawn, just not where the join
+      //                   looks. That is a join problem, and the pass id names
+      //                   the isolation that caused it.
+      //   elsewhere = 0 → NOTHING in the frame carries this layer id. The
+      //                   layer emitted no SharedQuadState at all, so no join
+      //                   could ever have matched it: the element is not in the
+      //                   drawn layer list, or its resource is not ready. That
+      //                   is a cc/producer problem, not a join problem.
+      size_t elsewhere = 0;
+      AggregatedRenderPassId elsewhere_pass;
+      if (canvas.sqs_layer_id != 0) {
+        for (const RootResourceQuad& cand : root_resource_quads_) {
+          if (!cand.from_root_resolved_pass &&
+              cand.sqs_layer_id == canvas.sqs_layer_id &&
+              cand.layer_namespace_id == canvas.layer_namespace_id) {
+            ++elsewhere;
+            elsewhere_pass = cand.pass_id;
+          }
+        }
+      }
       const char* reason =
           canvas.sqs_layer_id == 0
               ? "no layer_id stamped (legacy/TreesInViz channel or pre-Phase-1 "
                 "renderer)"
-              : "layer_id stamped but no root quad carries it (canvas quad "
-                "occluded, in a child pass, or resource not ready)";
+          : elsewhere > 0
+              ? "layer_id stamped and DRAWN, but only by a quad in a pass this "
+                "join does not search (cross-pass isolation)"
+              : "layer_id stamped but NO quad anywhere in the frame carries it "
+                "— the layer emitted no SharedQuadState (not in the drawn "
+                "layer "
+                "list, or resource not ready); no join could match it";
       static int fallback_dbg = 0;
       if ((fallback_dbg++ % 60) == 0) {
         VLOG(1) << "[DisplayXR] inline-3D ID-MATCH FALLBACK: reason=" << reason
                 << " id=" << canvas.id.ToString()
                 << " layer_id=" << canvas.sqs_layer_id
                 << " target=" << canvas.rect.ToString() << " among "
-                << root_resource_quads_.size() << " root quads";
+                << root_resource_quads_.size() << " quads ("
+                << cross_pass_diag_quads_
+                << " diagnostic-only), elsewhere=" << elsewhere << " pass="
+                << (elsewhere ? elsewhere_pass.GetUnsafeValue() : 0u);
       }
       // browser#117: the fallback compares on the COMMON visible space, so a
       // tile that is only partly on screen scores against its own quad exactly
diff --git a/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.h b/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.h
index 3b1618d..05e2810 100644
--- a/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.h
+++ b/components/viz/service/display/surface_aggregator.h
@@ -523,8 +523,23 @@ class VIZ_SERVICE_EXPORT SurfaceAggregator : public SurfaceObserver {
     // renderer did not stamp one (legacy channel / older renderer).
     uint32_t sqs_layer_id = 0;
     std::pair<uint32_t, uint32_t> layer_namespace_id;
+    // browser#117, DIAGNOSTIC ONLY. |pass_id| is the aggregated pass the quad
+    // was copied into; |from_root_resolved_pass| is whether it came out of a
+    // surface's ROOT resolved pass — the exact predicate that used to decide
+    // whether this quad was recorded at all. Every join admits candidates on
+    // |from_root_resolved_pass| and nothing else, so the matched set is
+    // unchanged; the two fields exist so the ID-MATCH FALLBACK marker can say
+    // whether an unmatched element's quad is NOWHERE or merely somewhere this
+    // join does not look. Those two need opposite follow-ups.
+    AggregatedRenderPassId pass_id;
+    bool from_root_resolved_pass = true;
   };
   std::vector<RootResourceQuad> root_resource_quads_;
+  // browser#117: how many diagnostic-only (non-root-resolved-pass) entries are
+  // in |root_resource_quads_|, capped so a pathological page cannot turn an
+  // observability aid into an unbounded per-frame allocation.
+  static constexpr size_t kMaxCrossPassDiagQuads = 512;
+  size_t cross_pass_diag_quads_ = 0;
 };
 
 }  // namespace viz
